<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ulnar neuropathy at the right elbow (cubital tunnel), post-traumatic (G56.21)</content>. About fifteen weeks after the injury, sensation has largely recovered (two-point discrimination 10 to 6 mm) but intrinsic weakness and clawing persist (first dorsal interosseous 3/5 to 3+/5; grip 20 to 24 kg against 38 kg on the left; QuickDASH 52 to 41). AAOS guidance advises surgery when nonsurgical treatment has not improved the condition or when compression has caused muscle weakness, and lost muscle may not recover if decompression is delayed. In shared decision-making, with Dr. Lindqvist-Obi&apos;s advice, he chose surgery now rather than three more months of splinting. [S]<list><item>In situ decompression of the ulnar nerve at the right elbow, booked with Dr. Lindqvist-Obi in three weeks; anterior transposition only if the nerve subluxes at operation.</item><item>Expected course explained: sensation and pain usually improve first; strength may recover over months and may not fully return.</item><item>Tendon transfers for the claw only if it persists a year or more after decompression.</item><item>Continue the anti-claw splint, the elbow pad, and hand therapy until surgery; he keeps working with his left hand.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Primary hypercholesterolemia (E78.00)</content>. LDL 82 mg/dL from 146 (44 percent lower) on atorvastatin 20 mg, within the 30 to 49 percent reduction expected of a moderate-intensity statin; no muscle symptoms. [S]<list><item>Continue atorvastatin 20 mg.</item><item>Lipid panel in a year once stable.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">History of falling (Z91.81)</content>. No further falls; handrail and lighting in place. [S]<list><item>Review at each visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ulnar motor and sensory examination: strength of the first dorsal interosseous, abductor digiti minimi, and deep flexor of the little finger (MRC grade), two-point discrimination of the little finger, Froment and Wartenberg signs, clawing, and wasting</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two-point discrimination 6 mm at the little finger; first dorsal interosseous 3+/5, abductor digiti minimi 4-/5, deep flexor 4+/5; clawing persists</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Grip and key-pinch dynamometry, both hands</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Grip 24 kg right, 38 kg left; key pinch 3.8 kg right</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Patient-reported hand function (QuickDASH)</td><td>Before treatment, then at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>41 of 100, from 52</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Splint use and avoiding pressure on and prolonged bending of the elbow</td><td>Every visit during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Both splints worn; elbow pad in use</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Hand therapy</td><td>Twice a week from diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fourteen sessions; continues until surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Surgical review at about three months for persistent weakness</td><td>At about three months from the injury</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Decompression chosen and booked</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Corticosteroid injection at the cubital tunnel</td><td>Not part of treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No benefit over placebo at three months in the Cochrane review</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel after starting a statin</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>LDL 82 mg/dL, 44 percent below baseline; total cholesterol 158, HDL 51, triglycerides 124 mg/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>ALT on a statin</td><td>Baseline only; then if symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Normal at baseline; no symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Statin adherence and muscle symptoms</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; no muscle aches</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, weight, and smoking status</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>124/74 mmHg; 73.6 kg; never smoker</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stroke excluded and the lesion localized by the second visit. Outcome by 2026-08-17: Normal neurological examination apart from the right ulnar territory; MRI brain normal; nerve conduction studies, EMG, and ultrasound confirm ulnar neuropathy at the elbow [S]</item><item>Keep working: drawing, writing, and teaching with the left hand, and painting with adapted tools and help. In progress; target 2027-02-01 [S]</item><item>Recovery of ulnar sensation and intrinsic strength, judged at about three months from the injury. In progress; target 2027-02-01 [S]</item><item>LDL down by 30 to 49 percent on a moderate-intensity statin. Outcome by 2026-10-20: LDL 146 to 82 mg/dL (44 percent) after nine weeks of atorvastatin 20 mg [S]</item><item>No further falls. In progress; target 2027-08-01 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Cubital Tunnel Syndrome (Ulnar Nerve Entrapment at the Elbow), OrthoInfo (2026); Treatment for ulnar neuropathy at the elbow (Caliandro P, et al.) (2025); Practice parameter for electrodiagnostic studies in ulnar neuropathy at the elbow: summary statement (American Association of Electrodiagnostic Medicine, American Academy of Neurology, American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation) (1999); 2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hyperlipidemia monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Surgery in three weeks; post-operative review with the hand surgeon at two weeks and primary care review after that; lipid panel in a year. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
